# How a magnifying glass really works
subtitle: "Trace two light rays through a lens to find the bigger letter, and see that what really magnifies is letting your eye get close."

## How a magnifying glass really works
use: ray_diagram
with:
    focal_length: 10
    object_distance: 7.5
    object_height: 4
    show_labels: false
    show_distances: true
    view:
      left: 32
      right: 22
      height: 17

    text t_fl "F" x 427.8 y 348 font_size 28 hidden true
    text t_fr "F" x 716.7 y 348 font_size 28 hidden true
    text t_letter "letter" #2f6b8f x 510 y 352 font_size 28 hidden true
    text t_image "image, 30 cm" x 178.9 y 638.3 font_size 28 hidden true
    icon eye size 56 x 630 y 375 of eye hidden true
    path np_tick #8a5fa8 points [[211.1,355],[211.1,395]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    text np_text "25 cm" #8a5fa8 x 211.1 y 330 font_size 28 hidden true
    group near members ["np_tick","np_text"] hidden true
    path bare #8a5fa8 points [[211.1,375],[211.1,433.8]] style.stroke_width 4 hidden true
    path bare_sight dashed #8a5fa8 points [[572.2,375],[211.1,433.8]] style.opacity 0.7 hidden true
    group bare_view members ["bare","bare_sight"] hidden true

    card title "How a magnifier works"

How does a magnifying glass make a letter look bigger, and where is the bigger letter? Here is the lens, edge-on.
    draw axis lens

Its focal length is ten centimetres: parallel light meets at F, ten centimetres out on either side.
    draw focal_left focal_right t_fl t_fr f_mark f_value parallel true

Hold a small letter 7.5 centimetres away, just inside the focal length.
    draw object t_letter do_mark do_value

A ray leaving the top of the letter parallel to the axis is bent through the far F.
    draw ray_parallel

A ray through the centre of the lens goes straight on.
    draw ray_center

Past the lens these two rays spread apart. They never meet, so there is no picture on this side for a screen to catch.
    highlight ray_parallel ray_center

But your eye can't know the rays were bent. It traces them straight back, to where they seem to come from.
    draw eye ray_parallel_ext ray_center_ext parallel true

They seem to come from here: an upright letter thirty centimetres behind the glass, four times as tall. A virtual image.
    draw image t_image

Four times as tall, but four times as far away: it fills the same angle as the letter itself, seen from 7.5 centimetres.
    highlight image

That is the trick. Your bare eye can't focus closer than about twenty-five centimetres, where the letter looks small.
    draw near bare_view

The lens moves the image out past that limit, so you see the letter sharp as if from 7.5 centimetres: over three times bigger.
    point at.ref image gesture circle

    erase t_image bare_view

    animate object_distance 8.5 duration 3

Slide the letter out to 8.5 centimetres: the image is now nearly seven times the letter's height, but 57 centimetres away.
    text t_far "← image 57 cm away" x 250 y 640 font_size 28

    erase t_far

Height over distance hardly changes. What magnifies is getting close; the lens only makes close focusable.
    animate object_distance 7.5 duration 2.5

    draw t_image

The near point creeps outward with age, which is why people past about forty start holding menus at arm's length.
    highlight near

    quiz "A stronger magnifier has a shorter focal length. Why does it magnify more?"
        right: It lets you hold the object closer: a shorter focal length puts the letter nearer your eye while its image stays far enough away to focus.
        + It lets you hold the object closer and still see it sharp
        * It makes the image taller at the same distance
        * It lets more light into your eye
---
